<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · 1901 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Kōjō no Tsuki</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ja">荒城の月</bdi> / <bdi lang="ja-Latn">Kōjō no Tsuki</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Japanese school song of 1901 about the moon shining over the ruins of an old castle.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Bansui Doi</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Rentarō Taki</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1901</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Doi Bansui (1901; he died in 1952))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojo-no-tsuki">Kōjō no Tsuki</a>&quot; (&quot;The Moon over the Ruined Castle&quot;) is a Japanese song of the Meiji period. The pianist and composer Rentarō Taki wrote the music in 1901 as a song for music lessons, to be sung without accompaniment, and it was printed in a songbook for junior high school pupils. The words are a poem by Bansui Doi. The ruins of different castles stand behind each part: the music was inspired by the ruins of Oka Castle, the poem by those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai-castle">Aoba Castle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsuruga-castle">Aizuwakamatsu Castle</a>.</p><p>Taki wrote the song in B minor. A slower D minor version by Kosaku Yamada, with accompaniment, is also widely sung. Taki's second bar has an E sharp, which the modern version usually sings as E, probably because the original did not fit traditional Japanese music.</p><p>The tenor Yoshie Fujiwara recorded the song in 1925 and was the first Japanese singer to make it known around the world. It later passed into jazz and rock: Thelonious Monk recorded it as &quot;Japanese Folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot; on his 1967 album Straight, No Chaser, and the German band Scorpions sang it as a power ballad at a Tokyo concert released on their 1978 live album Tokyo Tapes, one of the few versions that keeps close to Taki's original. Guitarists, pianists and singers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and abroad have recorded it since, and in 1998 the asteroid 8957 Koujounotsuki was named after it.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>As its title says, the song looks at the moon over a ruined castle, and its words were inspired by the ruins of real castles. The mood of the music, written in a minor key and often sung slowly and with nostalgia, is reflective and sad.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Japanese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Japanese</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">春高楼の花の宴</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Haru kōrō no hana no en</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In spring, the flower banquet in the high tower;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">巡る盃影さして</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">meguru sakazuki kage sashite</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the cups going round, with the light falling into them;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">千代の松が枝分け出でし</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">chiyo no matsu ga e wakeideshi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>through the branches of the thousand-year pines it came out:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">昔の光今いづこ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">mukashi no hikari ima izuko</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that light of old, where is it now?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">秋陣営の霜の色</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Aki jin'ei no shimo no iro</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In autumn, the colour of frost on the camp,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">鳴きゆく雁の数見せて</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">naki yuku kari no kazu misete</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>showing the count of the wild geese crying as they pass;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">植うる剣に照り沿ひし</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">uuru tsurugi ni teri soishi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it shone along the swords stood in the ground:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">昔の光今いづこ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">mukashi no hikari ima izuko</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that light of old, where is it now?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">今荒城の夜半の月</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Ima kōjō no yowa no tsuki</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now, the midnight moon over the ruined castle:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">変はらぬ光誰がためぞ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kawaranu hikari ta ga tame zo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its light, unchanged, for whom is it?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">垣に残るはただ葛</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kaki ni nokoru wa tada kazura</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What remains on the wall is only kudzu;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">松に歌ふはただ嵐</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">matsu ni utau wa tada arashi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>what sings in the pines is only the storm.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">天上影は変はらねど</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Tenjō kage wa kawaranedo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Above in heaven its light does not change, but</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">栄枯は移る世の姿</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">eiko wa utsuru yo no sugata</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>flourishing and withering pass on: the shape of the world.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">映さむとてか今も尚</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">utsusan to te ka ima mo nao</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is it to mirror this that even now, still,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">ああ荒城の夜半の月</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">ā kōjō no yowa no tsuki</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>ah, the midnight moon over the ruined castle?</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Doi Bansui (1901). Text from <a href="https://ja.wikisource.org/wiki/%E8%8D%92%E5%9F%8E%E3%81%AE%E6%9C%88" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (modified Hepburn).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: modified Hepburn of the words as they are read and sung; the historical kana spelling of the original is not transliterated letter by letter (沿ひし soishi, 変はらぬ kawaranu, 歌ふ utau, 映さむ utsusan, いづこ izuko). Kanji readings are the ones the song is sung with (高楼 kōrō, 陣営 jin'ei, 夜半 yowa, 栄枯 eiko, 天上 tenjō, 誰 ta).</li><li>'kage' (lines 1.2, 4.1) is the moon's light, not 'shadow' (its modern sense); 'the light of old' in the refrain is the same moonlight as it shone on the castle's past.</li><li>line 1.3: the subject of 'wakeideshi' (came out, parting [the branches]) is the moonlight, unnamed in the original; 'it' keeps that.</li><li>line 2.2: 'misete', the moonlight 'showing' (making visible) the number of the geese against the sky.</li><li>line 2.3: 'uuru tsurugi', literally 'planted swords': usually taken as swords thrust upright in the ground at the camp (or rows of swords standing like a planted field); the sense is not certain.</li><li>line 3.3: 'kaki', a fence or wall (here the castle's ruined wall); 'kazura', a creeping vine, taken as kudzu.</li><li>line 4.3: the subject is the moon: is it shining to reflect the world's rise and fall?</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="9nOhO7xlcvQ" data-title="Kōjō no Tsuki: Yumi Yamaguchi, soprano"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Yumi Yamaguchi, soprano</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel ユミと歌おう. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9nOhO7xlcvQ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kōjō no Tsuki, arranged by Kosaku Yamada</span><span class="sg-chain-h">slower arrangement in D minor with accompaniment</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Japanese Folk Song (Thelonious Monk, on Straight, No Chaser)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1967</span><span class="sg-chain-h">jazz arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La La Land (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2016</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Monk's version heard in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kōjō no Tsuki (Scorpions, on Tokyo Tapes)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1978</span><span class="sg-chain-h">rock power ballad, recorded live</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La luna sobre las ruinas del castillo (Eduardo Falú, on Palabras para mi guitarra)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1974</span><span class="sg-chain-h">guitar version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Montana: A Love Story (George Winston)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2004</span><span class="sg-chain-h">piano arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kōjō no Tsuki (Yngwie Malmsteen, Alcatrazz Japan Tour; Metallic Live DVD)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1984</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental, recorded live</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kōjō no Tsuki (Takeshi Terauchi &amp; Blue Jeans, on Let's Go Blue Jeans)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental rock cover</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cyberpunk 2077 (video game)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2020</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung by the character Hanako Arasaka</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Songs from the Silver Screen, Japanese release (Jackie Evancho)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2012</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in Japanese, bonus track</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">8957 Koujounotsuki (asteroid)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1998</span><span class="sg-chain-h">named after</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Poem by Bansui Doi</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1901 or earlier</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the words set by Taki</span></li></ul></section>
